            <ArticleTitle>New approaches in immunotherapy: allergen vaccination with immunostimulatory DNA.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>569-81, v</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Despite its success, conventional immunotherapy is saddled with a number of encumbrances. An allergenic vaccine that could reduce allergenicity, maintain immunogenicity, and be given in a few doses would have important therapeutic implications, as millions of patients with poorly controlled allergic rhinitis and asthma would be candidates for such a form of immunomodulation. This article addresses a specific adjuvant approach to immunotherapy in which highly active immunostimulatory phosphorothioate oligodeoxyribonucleotide moieties are linked to the principal allergenic moiety of a relevant aeroallergen.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
